        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Pressure-treated wood should be sealed. The treatment protects against rot and insects, but it does nothing to stop the water absorption, sun damage, cracking and warping that make a deck look old long before it is structurally worn out. A water-repellent finish slows all of that, and it costs little compared with replacing boards.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Timing is the part to get right. Wait until standard treated lumber passes the sprinkle test, which can take weeks to months, or seal kiln-dried after treatment lumber soon after installation. Brush end-cut preservative on every cut as you build, clean the deck before the first coat, and then keep up with a light recoat whenever the water stops beading.
          </p>
        </div>

